  "scenario": "A buyer is considering an inexpensive compact camera as a gift for a mother who wants simple garden and grandchildren snapshots. Frozen seller pages look encouraging but bind only to five exact listings: a Sony DSC-W80 old-model page at 49.95 dollars with a 91-percent aggregate over eleven reviews; an HP M447 camera-and-printer page at 60.61 dollars with a 100-percent aggregate over four reviews; a Canon ELPH 300 HS page at 129.95 dollars with an 82-percent aggregate over twelve reviews; a Kodak DX4530 page at 47.45 dollars with a 75-percent aggregate over twelve reviews; and a Sony W800 accessory-bundle page at 199 dollars with a 78-percent aggregate over twelve reviews. General pages describe point-and-shoot cameras as designed for simple operation, star ratings as rating scales, self-selection bias, real-user usability testing, subjective and objective image-quality assessment, optical aberration, shutter lag, and user versus professional review authorship. Three substantive community pages contain only scoped reports: one experienced switcher praises an iPhone camera's speed and results, another dislikes an iPhone's processing and would rather carry a DSLR, and a third seeks an older phone for a specific product-photo task. A fourth frozen forum URL reports low-end compact market displacement in its search title but has no useful captured body and is retained only in the registry, not as a critical proposition. No captured source establishes the delivered condition of any listing, the mother's actual usability, a same-scene comparison with her actual phone, an exact lens defect in a captured unit, a representative rating sample, or a universal winner between retailer raters and enthusiasts.",
