  "rules": {
    "seller_rating_offer_scope_v1": "The five frozen seller bodies expose only page-bound stock, SKU, aggregate rating, review count and price fields: Sony DSC-W80 SKU B000M4KXF6 at 49.95 dollars with 91 percent over eleven reviews; HP M447 SKU B0018N637W at 60.61 dollars with 100 percent over four reviews; Canon ELPH 300 HS SKU B004J41T7Q at 129.95 dollars with 82 percent over twelve reviews; Kodak DX4530 SKU B0000APONO at 47.45 dollars with 75 percent over twelve reviews; and Sony W800 bundle SKU B074DZM1J2 at 199 dollars with 78 percent over twelve reviews. These frozen fields do not reveal the star distribution, review recency, reviewer scenes, delivered condition, battery, accessories, return rights, lens performance, ease for the intended recipient or superiority to her actual phone.",
    "rating_population_self_selection_boundary_v1": "A star rating is a rating scale, not a standardized optical or usability test. Review sites may contain user or professional reviews, and self-selection can make responders differ from nonresponders. Therefore a seller aggregate describes only the frozen page's participating reviewers under unknown criteria. It can coexist with enthusiast criticism if the groups evaluate different devices, conditions, outputs or thresholds, but neither crowd transfers automatically to this mother and exact unit.",
    "point_shoot_recipient_usability_boundary_v1": "Point-and-shoot cameras are designed primarily for simple operation and are associated with easy snapshots by non-photographers, often using automatic focus, exposure and flash. That category intent does not prove that an old or bundled exact unit powers on reliably, focuses when needed, has acceptable menus, timing, grip, screen, battery, transfer or printing workflow for this recipient. Usability must be tested by observing the intended user performing intended tasks rather than merely collecting opinions.",
    "image_quality_lens_lag_scope_v1": "Image quality includes both system accuracy and visually significant attributes and may be assessed subjectively or objectively; those assessments need not agree. Optical aberrations can blur, distort or fringe an image, while shutter lag is the delay from trigger to recorded photograph and includes focus and metering lag. These mechanisms explain legitimate enthusiast test dimensions but do not diagnose any exact captured listing. Relevance depends on garden detail, indoor light, child motion, intended screen or print size, crop and the recipient's preference and timing success.",
    "community_phone_claim_scope_v1": "One experienced Android switcher reports an iPhone camera as near-instantaneous with pleasing results and easy editing. Another long-time iPhone user reports objectionable processing, skin tones and detail and says a DSLR would be preferable. A third asks which older iPhone fits an Etsy product-photo task because a Samsung phone performs poorly for that use. These are device-, author-, scene-, skill-, output- and time-scoped reports, not a controlled compact-versus-phone benchmark. Their conflict supports testing the mother's actual phone and priorities rather than choosing a crowd.",
    "feedback_reconciliation_matrix_v1": "Reconcile the apparently conflicting feedback by mapping every claim to exact device and condition, reviewer population, task, viewing size, metric and threshold. Seller satisfaction can be genuine for easy snapshots or value while enthusiast criticism can be genuine for pixel-level sharpness, aberration, lag, low light or motion. The overlap relevant to this gift is only what survives exact-offer verification and a recipient-matched garden, indoor, moving-child, output and workflow test; crowd size or technical vocabulary is not a tie-breaker.",
    "recipient_matched_trial_v1": "Verify one exact candidate's identity, condition, lens, battery, charger, card, cables, software or transfer path, warranty, return window and delivered cost. With the mother operating both that camera and her actual phone, repeat matched static garden detail, backlit scene, indoor person, moving-grandchild and flash or no-flash tasks. Normalize framing and final display or print size, mask or randomize device labels where practical, and separately record time to first shot, focus and timing hit rate, keeper rate, image preference, transfer success, charging, errors and willingness to carry. Predeclare stops and thresholds and do not infer a category rule from one lucky frame.",
    "camera_feedback_decision_preparation_v1": "Build a claim-to-task table and an exact-offer gate table. Keep seller aggregate, reviewer sample, category design intent, mechanism, community anecdote, current-condition verification, recipient trial and final decision in separate columns. Mark identity, condition, usability, timing, keeper, intended-output preference, transfer, carry, returnability and total cost pass, fail or unresolved. No rating, low price, enthusiast term, phone anecdote or isolated sharp crop compensates for a failed hard gate.",
    "evidence_bounded_compact_gift_decision_v1": "The packet proves no universal winner between retailer raters, enthusiasts, cheap compacts and phones. Buy only one verified exact returnable camera if the intended recipient can operate it, its repeated matched keeper and intended-output results meet predeclared thresholds, its workflow and carry burden pass, and it provides a meaningful advantage over her actual phone at acceptable delivered cost. Otherwise keep or improve the phone workflow, choose printing or handling support, run a corrected smaller trial, or defer. Report the decision only for the tested unit, recipient, scenes and outputs."
